161 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 119 0 R
/T (Olist sells across Brazil.)
/E (Olist sells across Brazil.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
162 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (Olist is a Brazilian marketplace. Thousands of small shops sell through it, and the platform takes care of the payment, the invoice and the delivery to the customer. The seller reaches the whole country without opening a single store, and Olist keeps a share of every sale. Because the platform carries the delivery, it also carries the promise that comes with it.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
163 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (In the period of this study, the platform sold R$ 13,449,529.68 of goods in 97,905 orders. Those orders travel from the big cities of the south east to small towns far away from them, so the business is spread over the whole map and not over one rich region.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
164 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 119 0 R
/E (The chart answers a first question: where does that money come from? A small group of product families, such as home goods, health and beauty, and technology, carries most of the value, and a long list of smaller families shares the rest. This matters for the rest of our story, because a delivery problem in one of the first families touches far more money than the same problem in the tail.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
165 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 119 0 R
/Pg 12 0 R
/A [435 0 R 0]
/Alt (01a_amounts_gmv_by_macro_category.png)
/K [5]
>>
endobj
166 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 119 0 R
/Pg 12 0 R
/A [436 0 R 0]
/K [6]
>>
endobj
167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 120 0 R
/Pg 13 0 R
/A [437 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
168 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 120 0 R
/T (From raw tables to answers.)
/E (From raw tables to answers.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
169 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (Olist publishes its data in 9 separate files: orders, order lines, payments, reviews, customers, sellers, products, categories and locations. On their own they answer almost nothing. We added how many people live in each state and a small calendar of sales events, and then we wrote a pipeline that reads, cleans, enriches, models, validates and loads the data, always in that order and with no manual step.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
170 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (The result is the model in the picture: one central table with the facts of each order line and 7 short tables around it for dates, customers, sellers, products, states, payment types and order status. The pipeline also writes a quality log, so every rule we applied and every row we changed can be read later. The data covers purchases from 2017-01-01 to 2018-08-31.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
171 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 120 0 R
/E (We chose Power BI for the dashboard because it reads this shape of model well, it draws maps without extra tools, and our team already had it on Windows at no extra cost. Python stays for the statistics, the confidence intervals and the charts that a dashboard does not do.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
172 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 120 0 R
/Pg 13 0 R
/A [438 0 R 0]
/Alt (04_model_view.png)
/K [5]
>>
endobj
173 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 120 0 R
/Pg 13 0 R
/A [439 0 R 0]
/K [6]
>>
endobj
174 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 121 0 R
/Pg 14 0 R
/A [440 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
175 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 121 0 R
/T (The delivery promise, and how it breaks.)
/E (The delivery promise, and how it breaks.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
176 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 121 0 R
/E (When a customer buys on the platform, the website shows an estimated delivery date. That date is the promise of the company, and it is the date the customer remembers and waits for. So our question is not only how fast the delivery is, but whether the company keeps what it said.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
177 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 121 0 R
/E (The chart compares two things for each region: how many days a delivery really takes, and the average time that was promised. In the south and the south east the two are close, and most parcels land inside the promise. The farther the customer lives from the sellers, the wider the curve becomes, and the more orders fall outside it.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
178 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 121 0 R
/E (At first sight the picture looks calm. The average review of the whole period is 4.11 out of five, so most customers are happy with what they received. But 6.8% of the orders we can check arrive after the promised date. That share is small, and it is easy to ignore in a monthly report. The rest of this presentation follows it, because behind that number there is a cost that nobody has written down yet.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 121 0 R
/Pg 14 0 R
/A [441 0 R 0]
/Alt (03_kde_delivery_by_region.png)
/K [5]
>>
endobj
