/Pg 18 0 R
/A [452 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
200 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 125 0 R
/T (Where it happens: North and Northeast.)
/E (Where it happens: North and Northeast.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E (If late orders were spread evenly over the country, this would be a national problem with no clear owner and no clear fix. The data says they are not spread evenly at all.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
202 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E (In the Nordeste region, 12.8% of the orders arrive after the promised date. In Norte the share is 8.6%, and in Centro-Oeste 6.5%. The regions of the south and the south east, where most sellers are, stay below all of them. Each bar in the chart carries a short line that shows how sure we are of its value, and the distance between Nordeste and the rest is much wider than that doubt.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
203 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 125 0 R
/E (This turns a vague worry into an address. The company does not need to fix its delivery everywhere: it needs to look at the routes that end far from the sellers, where the parcel changes hands more times and the estimated date was built with the same rule as a short city delivery. It also tells us who should be in the room when the next delivery plan is written, because the answer lives in logistics and not in marketing.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
204 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 125 0 R
/Pg 18 0 R
/A [453 0 R 0]
/Alt (06_errorbars_late_rate_region.png)
/K [5]
>>
endobj
205 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
