208 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 126 0 R
/E (Inside those regions we can go one step closer and look at single states, because a region is still a large area with very different roads and cities.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
209 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 126 0 R
/E (Three states stand clearly above the rest. In AL, 21.5% of the orders arrive late. MA follows with 17.5%, and SE with 15.4%. All three are in the north east of the country, the same area the regional view pointed to, so the two views agree with each other.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
210 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 126 0 R
/E (One word about method, because it matters for trust. A state with only a handful of delivered orders could look terrible by pure chance, so we only keep the states that have enough checked orders to be fair. The chart also sorts the states instead of painting a single colour over a map, which makes the ranking easy to read for anyone. What we have here is a short list of places where a change would be felt by many customers at once. With a list this short, a pilot can start in one state, be measured against the others, and only then be extended to the rest.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
