/T (Distance and freight explain part of it.)
/E (Distance and freight explain part of it.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
219 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (Before asking for money or for new warehouses, we wanted to understand why distance hurts so much. Two things show up clearly in the data.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
220 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (First, the freight of an order grows with the distance between the seller and the customer, but the link is not mechanical: the rank correlation is 0.59, so distance explains part of the price and weight, size and the seller own rules explain the rest. Second, freight weighs much more on the bill far from the south east. In Norte the freight of an order is 0.36 of the price of the goods, while in Sudeste it is only 0.21.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
221 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 128 0 R
/E (So the long routes are the expensive ones and also the slow ones, and they are exactly the routes where the promised date is missed. This is good news for the plan, because it means the problem is not random. It follows the map, and anything that makes those routes shorter, cheaper or better estimated attacks the cause instead of the symptom.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
222 0 obj
<<
