225 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 129 0 R
/T (Sales peaks add pressure, not value.)
/E (Sales peaks add pressure, not value.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (Distance is not the only thing that pushes a delivery over its date. The calendar pushes too, and the chart shows the clearest example of the period.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (The Black Friday week of 2017 brought 2,972 orders, against 1,001 in a normal week. That is 3.0x the usual work for the same delivery team, in the same days, with the same promise written on every product page. And the money each order brings does not grow with the volume: the average ticket of that week is R$ 159.02, almost the same as the R$ 159.49 of a median week.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (This is an important detail for the business. A peak like this does not make the company richer per sale, so the extra sales do not pay for the extra stress by themselves. It only concentrates work, and it does so on a date that everybody knows months in advance. A peak that can be seen coming is a peak that can be planned. Capacity can be hired for those days, stock can be moved closer before them, and the promised date can be made longer only while the peak lasts.)
/Pg 22 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
